nal beyond what the LF heuristics captured. The TLM negative finding stays intact. Now I'm looking at the gold annotation setup: 650 windows from SWE-agent with high annotator agreement, showing a stagnation rate of about 31.5% versus the 26.6% in the majority-vote labels. Now I'm looking at how the gradient boosting model performs on the gold labels, showing similar results to the random forest with a macro F1 of 0.80 and a slightly smaller performance drop of 19.5 percentage points. The probe models using smaller language models are performing better overall—the Qwen model achieves 0.82 macro F1 with much less degradation when moving from the LF labels to gold, suggesting it's learning more robust patterns. Now I'm looking at how different approaches compare on the gold standard—the TWV labels show a 7 percentage point drop compared to majority vote, while the probe trained on TWV labels reaches 0.80 macro F1. The structural approach underperforms the probes by 2-4 points, but when I examine cases where the structural and probe methods disagree, the probe shows a significant 14 point advantage.\n\nLooking at the error patterns, the probe has a much higher false positive rate on the weak labels that are actually correct in gold (42% vs 8%), which suggests it's learning something different than the structural approach. The core issue seems to be that the weak label functions systematically miss semantically diverse stagnation cases—94 out of 143 disagreements are instances where the weak labels say progress but gold says stagnation, because the label functions don't capture when an agent is doing varied but ultimately ineffective actions. The structural methods collapse from near-perfect performance to 0.79-0.80 because they've essentially memorized the weak label function mapping rather than learning the underlying task.\n\nThe probe degrades less severely (0.906 to 0.82, an 8.6 percentage point drop) because its learned representations capture semantic patterns beyond what the heuristic label functions encode—and interestingly, the probe's false positive pattern of predicting stagnation when the weak labels say progress actually partially aligns with what gold considers correct. On windows where the weak labels and gold disagree, the probe outperforms the structural approach by about 14 percentage points, suggesting those \"errors\" on weak labels were partially right all along. The LLM judge zero-shot actually improves on gold by 6.8 percentage points because some of its disagreements with the weak labels were genuinely correct human judgments, while the temporal smoothing in the TWV labels reinforces the weak function patterns that gold rejects, making them worse than the majority vote baseline.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics across these methods on the gold-validated set. Now I'm looking at how different methods perform specifically on the disagreement cases, where the structural approaches drop to around 38-40% F1 while the probe and LLM judge methods handle them better at 48-52%. I'm also preparing a scatter plot visualization that shows how each method's performance degrades compared to the gold standard, with the structural methods showing the steepest decline.\n\nThe key insight emerging is that the structural features were benefiting from a circular evaluation artifact — they looked dominant when evaluated against the original labeling function outputs, but when tested against the gold standard, the probe methods actually outperform them by a significant margin. The structural approaches lose about 20 percentage points while probes only drop 8-10 points, which suggests the structural features were essentially capturing labeling function behavior rather than genuine stagnation detection.\n\nWhat's particularly revealing is that the probe's false positives on the labeling function labels — those 42% of cases — actually align with the gold standard, indicating the probe has learned to detect real semanti