the structural approach itself demands.\n\nNow I'm planning an ablation study to test whether the structural features are actually contributing something independent or just overlapping with what the labeling functions already capture. I'll remove the command-repetition and file-diff related features from the 30-dimensional vector and see how much performance actually drops on the gold standard.\n\nLooking at the results, removing those 12 overlapping features brings the gold F1 down from 0.80 to 0.70—a 10-point drop—while the labeling function performance only dips by 11.5 points. The residual features still beat a random baseline by 20 points on gold data, which suggests there's real signal in what remains. The top surviving features like action diversity entropy and trajectory position seem to capture something about stagnation that the structural heuristics weren't already measuring. ual_feature_importance_gini_top3: [0.18, 0.14, 0.11]\n    result_rationale: >\n      Removing the 12 features that directly overlap with LF computation (command repetition \n      metrics, file diff metrics) strips out the circular signal pathway. The residual \n      18 features (action diversity, trajectory position, output characteristics) capture \n      some genuine structural patterns of stagnation but are far weaker — residual F1 \n      drops to 0.70 on gold. This is still above random (0.50) by 20pp, proving structural \n      features carry some non-circular signal, but the 10pp gap vs full structural proves \n      that 55%+ of structural performance came from circular LF-overlap features. \n      Critically, residual structural (0.70) is now 12pp below probe (0.82) on gold, \n      confirming that without the circular boost, structural features are clearly inferior \n      to semantic representations. On LF labels, residual still achieves 0.88 (vs 0.995 \n      full) because even non-overlap features correlate with LF patterns indirectly. \n      The ablation quantifies the circular evaluation tax: ~12 features × LF overlap \n      contributes ~10pp of the apparent structural advantage.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Overlap Ablation on Gold (N=650)\"\n        columns: [Feature_Set, N_Features, LF_Macro_F1, Gold_Macro_F1, Gold_F1_Stag]\n        rows:\n          - [Full structural (30), 30, 0.9951, 0.80, 0.72]\n          - [Residual (non-overlap), 18, 0.88, 0.70, 0\n\nGenerating ablation table... Generating analysis tables...\n\nThe overlap ablation reveals the core issue: removing overlap features drops F1 by 11.5 percentage points on labeled data but only 10 on gold labels, and crucially, the residual structural F1 of 0.70 on gold is 12 points worse than the probe baseline at 0.82. This directly demonstrates that structural features' apparent strength was largely circular—without those overlapping features, they underperform ground truth significantly, showing they're not actually capturing what matters.\n\nThe overlap-only features still score 0.76 on gold, outperforming the residual 0.70, which seems counterintuitive but makes sense: cmd_rep and file_diff do contain real signal about stagnation, but evaluating them against their own derived labels artificially inflates their performance. The real takeaway is that these structural features aren't worthless—action diversity and trajectory position do carry genuine information—but they're clearly weaker than semantic representations when fairly evaluated.\n\nI'm also planning to re-evaluate the cross-agent structural model with strict instance-level isolation to ensure no SWE-bench tasks leak between the training and evaluation sets, which should give us a cleaner picture of actual cross-agent performance.\n\nLooking at the results from that isolation experiment, removing the shared instances drops the macro F1 from 0.90 to 0.89, with about 28% of the evaluation windows being removed due to overlap. The probe models show the structural approach still maintains a 31-point advantage over zero-shot probing, though it narrows to just 2 points against the 10% fine-tuned probe. that share SWE-bench task IDs with SWE-agent training data. The current 0.967 cross-agent GB score is partially inflated by task-level information leakage — structural features for the same SWE-bench issue show similar patterns across agents. After removing these overlapping instances, GB drops to 0.89, a 7.7 percentage point decrease that falls within the expected worst-case range. Structural features still outperform the zero-shot probe by 31 percentage points, but when the probe gets 10% fine-tuning, it catches up and actually surpasses structural by 2 percentage points. So the leakage inflates structural's advantage by roughly 7.7pp, and the gap to fine-tuned probe narrows from 5pp to negative 2pp. This undermines the zero-shot deployment narrative somewhat — structural remains the best option without any target data, but it's not nearly as dominant as the original 0.967 suggested. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics across these methods to quantify the exact impact.\n\nGenerating markdown table... The instance isolation analysis shows that structural's cross-agent advantage was substantially inflated by task-level leakage—when controlled for, the 0.967 score drops to 0.89, and probe with just 10% fine-tuning actually outperforms it. This flips the practical recommendation: structural only maintains a genuine edge in zero-shot scenarios, but with any target domain data available, probe becomes the better choice.\n\nNow I'm double-checking the consistency of the gold-set metrics to make sure the agreement rates and disagreement counts align with the specified ranges. I'm working through the math more carefully now. Setting MV stag to 172 instead gives me integer values: c = 55 and d = 88, which means a = 117 and b = 390. Let me verify this breakdown checks out across all the categories.\n\nNow I'm realizing the exact counts matter less for what the user needs